 in the very early stages. I am playing as \'@\' and currently have 1 piece on the board against the opponent\'s 4 pieces. The opponent controls the center and has extended slightly downwards to F5. My available moves are (D, 6), (F, 4), and (F, 6).\n\nEvaluation: \n1. Move (D, 6): Flips the piece at D5. This creates a horizontal line of my pieces (D4, D5, D6). However, it extends my presence towards the right and might allow the opponent to respond with moves like G5, expanding the game boundaries significantly.\n2. Move (F, 6): Flips the piece at E5 diagonally. This places a piece further out from the main cluster (at 6,6), which is generally less optimal in the opening as it increases the frontier too quickly.\n3. Move (F, 4): Flips the piece at E4. This creates a vertical line of my pieces (D4, E4, F4) mirroring the opponent\'s setup. This is a "quiet" move that keeps my pieces grouped centrally and minimizes the number of discs flipped, which restricts the opponent\'s mobility and valid options in the next turn.\n\nReason: (F, 4) is the most strategic choice for the opening. It allows me to regain central influence while keeping the board state compact. By making a quiet move that minimizes the frontier, I limit the opponent\'s ability to make explosive moves or gain positional advantage on the edges/corners this early in the game.\n\nAnswer: (F, 4)'}
