rms.  It\'s the best option here as it limits the opponent and consolidates my position.\n\n\n', 'output': "Situation: The game is in the early stages. The opponent (White, *) has a cluster of pieces in the center (D3-D5, E4-E5, F5), while I (Black, @) have a single piece at F4. It is my turn to play.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **Move (C, 4)**: This move sandwiches the opponent's pieces at D4 and E4 between C4 and my existing piece at F4. It flips two pieces, creating a vertical line of Black pieces from C4 to F4. This is a central move that strengthens my control of the middle of the board. By flipping D4 and E4 to Black, I also remove potential anchors the opponent could use to play on the right side (Column 6), restricting their mobility.\n2.  **Move (D, 6)**: This move sandwiches E5 with F4. It places a piece on the outside edge of the current cluster. While valid, it extends my frontier to the right and allows the opponent easy responses (like E6 or F6) to flip the piece back immediately.\n3.  **Move (F, 6)**: This move sandwiches F5 with F4. Similar to D6, it places a piece on the edge. This invites the opponent to play in the G row (e.g., G4, G5), which creates a messy edge battle and gives the opponent opportunities to flank.\n\nReason: Move (C, 4) is the strongest choice strategically. It adheres to the principle of centralization, keeping my pieces grouped in the middle rather than extending to the edges prematurely. By converting the internal pieces D4 and E4, I disrupt the opponent's connectivity and limit their valid responses on the right side of the board, forcing them into a more confined game on the left.\n\nAnswer: (C, 4)"}
