ing at the other moves, many of them can open the game up and give the opponent more chances to take spaces. B8 locks down a section. B8 builds up the edge. That's the move! The move at (B, 8) builds up the pieces in a very strong way. It extends my territory and it locks down the board.\n\n**Answer:** (B, 8)\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: The game is in the mid-to-late phase. I possess a significant advantage by controlling the entire top edge (Row A), including both corners A1 and A8. The opponent's pieces are concentrated in the center and lower-right quadrant. The right edge has an opening at B8 adjacent to my corner A8.\n\nEvaluation: The move (B, 8) is strategically superior. It places a piece on the right edge connected immediately to my established corner at A8. This move captures a horizontal line of opponent pieces along Row B (B3 to B7). Because I already control the entire Row A immediately above, the newly placed piece at B8 and the captured pieces along Row B are extremely stable; the opponent cannot flank them from the top. Other moves like (G, 8) involve playing to a C-square adjacent to an empty corner (H8), which is risky as it might surrender the corner to the opponent.\n\nReason: (B, 8) is a safe, stable extension of my existing territory. It solidifies control over the top-right sector of the board and limits the opponent's counter-play options, whereas other moves are either less impactful internal flips or risky moves near empty corners.\n\nAnswer: (B, 8)"}
