erous F8/H8 corridor.\n\nNow I am going to check **F1**. It\'s an edge move on the left. It flips a few pieces diagonally. I have G1 and B1... so, if I take F1, what is the flip direction? Is this move safe for me? I am making sure not to give the opponent access to the corners here, too.\n\n**F6** flips a *lot* of pieces on the board! I decide to avoid moves with such huge consequences.\n\n**G2** is on a C-Square. I always try to avoid this, right? Not unless there is no way around it!\n\n**G3, G5, G6**. I decide to avoid.\n\n**G8**: Is a no-go. I am making this list of risky squares to avoid!\n\n**Edge vs. Edge**\n\nI circle back to **C8** and **F1** because they both seem strong. C8 extends a line. F1 is isolated. Which is better? With F1 I check the flips again.\n\nChecking **C8** I want to double-check my logic. A8-B8 is solid, and adding C8 reinforces that. I\'m thinking about which edge move gives the opponent fewer choices.\n\nWith **F1**, the diagonal flip. Is it *good*? Is H1 safe? Yes, F1 looks to be a good move.\n\nMy final thought is that **C8** might be the better play because it adds to my already strong edge.\n\n**C8: The Choice**\n\nFinally, I weigh everything. C8 *is* a move! It extends my edge and keeps the game "quiet." No massive flips or opening up new fronts for the opponent. Building on a solid foundation is the safest bet for me right now.\n\nI double-check the notation: the coordinate for C8. Ready. The answer is **(C, 8)**.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the late middle stage. I have a dominant position in the top rows (A and B) and control two corners (A1, A8). The opponent controls the center-bottom area. My strategy should be to consolidate my territory by securing edges and limiting the opponent\'s mobility (making "quiet" moves that don\'t flip too many discs).\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(C, 8)**: This is a strong edge move. It places a piece at C8, which is adjacent to my existing stable pieces at A8 and B8. It flips C6 and C7