ocking the opponent. G6 does not give corners to opponent. Thus, G6.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the endgame phase with 14 empty squares remaining. I possess a strong position on the top edge (row A) and the center, while the opponent controls the bottom edge (row H). The key is to make a stable move that increases my piece count without giving the opponent access to the corners or unstable edges.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (G, 2): Flips several pieces but is unstable. The opponent can reply with (G, 3) immediately, using their piece at G1 to flip G2 back, resulting in a loss of position.\n- Move (E, 7): Flips two pieces but creates a diagonal alignment (D8-E7-F6) that allows the opponent to play at (G, 5) and capture E7.\n- Move (F, 8): A C-square move near the empty H8 corner. While it flips F7 and seems momentarily safe, playing C-squares when the corner is empty is generally strategically risky.\n- Move (G, 6): Flips three opponent pieces (F5, E4, D3) along the diagonal leading to C2. This move is stable as it does not allow the opponent to immediately recapture the piece or access the adjacent empty spots on the H-row (H6, H7) effectively. It also blocks potential opponent moves in the G-row.\n\nReason: (G, 6) is the best choice because it is a stable internal move that flips a good number of pieces (3) and consolidates my control in the center. Unlike the other options, it does not offer the opponent an immediate tactical counterattack or easy access to the corners.\n\nAnswer: (G, 6)'}
