ight quadrant (rows G and H).\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (G, 2) flips pieces on column F and row E but allows the opponent to play at G3 immediately, sandwiching the new piece against G1 and recapturing it.\n- Move (G, 3) is a high-flip move that impacts the center and row G. However, it opens up the board significantly and enables the opponent to access H7 by flipping G6.\n- Move (H, 6) is tactically weak; it places a piece adjacent to an empty H7. The opponent can respond by playing H7, which would sandwich and flip the piece at H6, gaining a strong edge position.\n- Move (H, 7) places a piece adjacent to my existing corner at H8. This is a stable, quiet move that consolidates the edge. While it flips G6 (giving the opponent a potential move at G3 or H6), retaining the H7-H8 block is positionally superior.\n\nReason: Move (H, 7) is the most strategic choice because it builds a stable block of pieces connected to the H8 corner. It avoids the immediate tactical pitfalls of the other moves (like immediate recapture or surrendering the H7 square to the opponent) and preserves a strong edge presence.\n\nAnswer: (H, 7)'}
